.TH "DOCUMENT.CREATEELEMENTNS" "3JS" "April 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Document.createElementNS \- Document: createElementNS() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]createElementNS()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Document\f[R] interface creates a new element with the specified
namespace URI and qualified name.
.PP
This is useful in mixed\-namespace documents, such as SVG or MathML
embedded in HTML, where the parser cannot reliably infer the namespace.
.PP
The \f[CR]createElement()\f[R] method is simpler if you want to create a
plain HTML element.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
createElementNS(namespaceURI, qualifiedName)
createElementNS(namespaceURI, qualifiedName, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]namespaceURI\f[R]
A string that specifies the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] to associate with
the element.
Some important namespace URIs are:
.RS
.TP
HTML
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\f[R]
.TP
SVG
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\f[R]
.TP
MathML
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]qualifiedName\f[R]
A string containing the qualified name of the new element.
The \f[CR]nodeName\f[R] property of the created element is initialized
with this value.
.RS
.PP
The format of the qualified name is \f[CR]prefix:localName\f[R] or
\f[CR]localName\f[R], where the parts are defined as:
.TP
\f[B]prefix\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A \(lqshort alias\(rq for the namespace.
The prefix is optional, but if it is specified the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] parameter must also be specified.
If the prefix is set to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] must be set to
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R], respectively.
.RS
.PP
The value is used to initialize the new element\(cqs \f[CR]prefix\f[R]
property.
Defaults to \f[CR]null\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]localName\f[R]
The local name of the element.
The value is used to initialize the new element\(cqs
\f[CR]localName\f[R] property.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object with the following optional properties (note that only one of
\f[CR]is\f[R] and \f[CR]customElementRegistry\f[R] may be set):
.RS
.TP
\f[B]is\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A string defining the tag name for a custom element previously defined
using \f[CR]customElements.define()\f[R].
The new element will be given an \f[CR]is\f[R] attribute whose value is
the custom element\(cqs tag name.
.TP
\f[B]customElementRegistry\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A \f[CR]CustomElementRegistry\f[R] that sets the Scoped custom element
registry of a custom element.
For backward compatibility, some browsers allow you to pass a string
here instead of an object, where the string\(cqs value is the custom
element\(cqs tag name.
See \c
.UR https://web.dev/articles/web-components
Extending native HTML elements
.UE \c
\ for more information on how to use this parameter.
.RE
.SS Return value
The new \f[CR]Element\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]NamespaceError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] value is:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
not a valid namespace URI.
.IP \(bu 2
set to the empty string when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] has a value.
.IP \(bu 2
not the value \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R] when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] is set
to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], respectively.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]InvalidCharacterError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if either the \f[CR]prefix\f[R] or \f[CR]localName\f[R] is not
valid:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]prefix\f[R] must have at least one character, and cannot
contain ASCII whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]>\f[R]
(U+0000, U+002F, or U+003E, respectively).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]localName\f[R] is a valid element name if it has a length of
at least 1 and:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
it starts with an alphabet character and does not contain ASCII
whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]>\f[R] (U+0000,
U+002F, or U+003E, respectively).
.IP \(bu 2
it starts with \f[CR]:\f[R] (U+003A), \f[CR]_\f[R] (U+005F), or any
characters in the range U+0080 to U+10FFFF (inclusive), \f[I]and\f[R]
the remaining code points only include those same characters along with
the ASCII alphanumeric characters, \f[CR]\-\f[R] (U+002D), and
\f[CR].\f[R] (U+002E),
.RE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Earlier versions of the specification were more
restrictive, requiring that the \f[CR]qualifiedName\f[R] be a valid \c
.UR https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#dt-name
XML name
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]NotSupportedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if both the \f[CR]is\f[R] and \f[CR]customElementRegistry\f[R]
options are specified.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
This shows how to create a new \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element in the XHTML
namespace.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] divElementXHTML = document.createElementNS(
  \(dqhttp://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\(dq,
  \(dqdiv\(dq,
);

\f[I]// This is equivalent!\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] divElementHTML = document.createElement(\(dqdiv\(dq);
.EE
.SS Create an SVG element
This example shows how you might create an SVG element
(\f[CR]SVGSVGElement\f[R]) and append it to the HTML \f[CR]<body>\f[R]
element.
.PP
Using \f[CR]createElementNS()\f[R] with the SVG namespace is necessary
when working with an HTML document.
If you were to call \f[CR]createElement(\(dqsvg\(dq)\f[R], an
\f[CR]HTMLUnknownElement\f[R] would be returned, and the SVG would not
be rendered.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] svgNS = \(dqhttp://www.w3.org/2000/svg\(dq;

\f[B]const\f[R] svg = document.createElementNS(svgNS, \(dqsvg\(dq);
svg.setAttribute(\(dqwidth\(dq, \(dq100\(dq);
svg.setAttribute(\(dqheight\(dq, \(dq100\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] circle = document.createElementNS(svgNS, \(dqcircle\(dq);
circle.setAttribute(\(dqcx\(dq, \(dq50\(dq);
circle.setAttribute(\(dqcy\(dq, \(dq50\(dq);
circle.setAttribute(\(dqr\(dq, \(dq40\(dq);
circle.setAttribute(\(dqfill\(dq, \(dqsteelblue\(dq);

svg.appendChild(circle);
document.body.appendChild(svg);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]document.createElement()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]document.createTextNode()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.namespaceURI\f[R]
